[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination: inspect oral mucosa (lips, buccal mucosa, tongue, palate) for papules, plaques, or ulcers; inspect urethral meatus and coronal sulcus for lesions; palpate epitrochanteric lymph nodes.

[THOUGHT] The request for additional physical exam components (oral mucosa, urethral meatus, coronal sulcus, epitrochanteric nodes) returned “Not specified”, meaning those findings are unavailable in this clinical environment — likely due to lack of access or documentation. Since all eight evidence domains must be *systematically addressed*, and I have now attempted to gather data across each:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — established (non-healing penile + finger sores);  
2) Course — partially clarified (chronic, treatment-resistant);  
3) Associated symptoms — negative systemic/mucocutaneous findings;  
4) Past medical history — no recalled immunosuppression;  
5) Medications/allergies — no active meds or known allergies;  
6) Social/exposure — unrecalled, but biopsy + serology constrain differential;  
7) Targeted physical exam — core features obtained (lesion morphology, no lymphadenopathy); missing elements unavailable;  
8) Lab/imaging — biopsy histopathology + syphilis/HIV serology completed; special stains and HPV PCR unavailable.

The most consistent diagnosis given *all available evidence* is **bowenoid papulosis**, an HPV-driven, premalignant condition that commonly presents as persistent, pigmented or erythematous, crusted plaques on genitalia and can autoinoculate to fingers. It is often misdiagnosed as syphilis, explains the negative serology and treatment failure, and aligns perfectly with the biopsy findings (multinucleation, hyperchromasia, mitoses).

